Abstract: The development of efficient, low-cost electrocatalysts as alternatives to RuO2/IrO2-based systems for the oxygen evolution reaction (OER) remains challenging, primarily due to the slow reaction kinetics, the need for high activity and stability, and the high cost and low abundance of noble metals. In this study, pure Mo2C electrocatalysts and iron-doped Mo2C electrocatalysts (1, 3, and 5 wt%) were synthesized via a gas-solid reaction using H2/CH4 as the reaction atmosphere, starting from ammonium heptamolybdate and iron nitrate, followed by heat treatment at 700 °C for 2 h. X-ray diffraction analysis revealed a single-phase Mo2C in the samples containing 0, 1 and 3% Fe, whereas the sample with 5% Fe contained mainly Mo2C and a small amount of metallic iron (a-Fe), as confirmed by Mössbauer spectroscopy. SEM analyses indicated particles with irregular morphology and a heterogeneous size distribution. XPS confirmed the presence of Mo, Fe, C, and O, having Mo and Fe several oxidation states. The Raman spectra revealed the presence of the ß-Mo2C phase. In electrocatalytic tests conducted in an alkaline medium, increasing the Fe concentration increased the overpotential reduced from 346 mV to 268 mV at 20 mA cm?² and significantly decreased the charge-transfer resistance from 21.0 to 1.9 O cm-2. The catalysts also exhibited stability for 24 h. These outcomes indicate that the incorporation of Fe into Mo2C improves electrocatalytic activity and makes the material promising for OER.

