Abstract: A fused dihydropyrrolidino-pyrimidine hit with low lipophilicity and excellent ligand efficiency was identified in a biochemical screen of the Global Health Chemical Diversity Library (GHCDL) against Plasmodium lysyl-tRNA synthetase (KRS). Structure-guided lead optimization delivered analogues with potent parasite growth inhibition, excellent biochemical and cellular selectivity (>1000-fold), and oral efficacy in the malaria NOD-scid-IL2R?null (SCID) mouse model. Structural information and computational methods were deployed to identify a potent and selective basic KRS inhibitor (30) with an extended half-life to reduce the dose regimen to a single-dose cure. Compound 30 displayed a long half-life across preclinical species, favorable safety, and activity across Plasmodium species as well as against drug-resistant and sensitive P. falciparum strains and field isolates. Unfortunately, 30 lacked oral bioavailability, which could not be mitigated with a prodrug approach. Nevertheless, learnings from this series will assist future KRS programs in delivering a clinical candidate with this novel mode of action.

